Another good idea.  Instead of just having monsters with generic names have
each group type have dungeon-level-frequency-probabilities associated with the
monster's (personal) level.

For instance :

On level 1 it would be unlikely that you'd find a Kobold higher than level
2 or 3 but as you go down the Kobold becomes more rare, yet more likely to
be a higher level.  On level 10 there would only be a 10% chance of finding
a kobold but he would be like a Level 10-14 Kobold (or something like that).

Also, in addition to having different types of each monsters (ie-Red, Golden
Naga) let there be a file that stores the monster level titles for each
type.

For instance :

All Kobold's would have the titles :  LEVEL   TITLE
                                       1-3    Private
                                       4-5    Sergeant
                                       6-7    Colonel
                                       8-9    General
                                       10-12  King
                                       13-    Emperor

This would be easy (I imagine) because the monsters level would be stored
in a variable and that variable (and monster type, of course) would only
need to be cross referenced to the correct file (ie-Kobolds) and have the
correspnding level title picked out and slapped onto that particular monster.

Of course, higher level monsters would gain hit points (not different abilities
though, that would be too corny)  However, each level would have innate
abilities, like a King Kobold might have spells or a Colonel Kobold might
be able to summon other Private Kobolds, etc.

Higher levels would also mean lower AC too.

What do you all think?

